[4] The determinative issue in this claim was the existence of a viable internal flight alternative (IFA) in Roatan.
ANALYSIS
Identity

[5] The claimant's personal identity as a national of Honduras is established on a balance of probabilities by her genuine Honduran passport3 and her U.S. biometrics4.
Credibility

[6] The claimant was a credible witness on a balance of probabilities. Her evidence was consistent with her narrative and was generally supported by documentary evidence and the country conditions documents.

[7] As such, I accept her allegations and summarize my findings briefly below:
a) The claimant and her spouse were pastors at a Catholic Church in Honduras. In XXXX 2018, they began seeing unknown persons sitting at the back of the Church. They later identified themselves as members of the Barrio 18 gang and left the church when asked without incident;
b) On the afternoon of XXXX XXXX, 2018 while the claimant and her spouse were away, members of the gang attended their house and began asking her nephews about the claimant, specifically what her surname was. Her nephews lied and gave her maiden name of XXXX;
c) The next day the gang members returned and entered the home without permission. The gang told the nephews that if they didn't get information on the whereabouts of the claimant's brother, they would kill the entire family;
d) Several years prior, the claimant's nephew XXXX XXXX was murdered by the gang in retaliation for the claimant's brother XXXX XXXX naming a high-ranking Barrio 18 member as a perpetrator of a vehicle theft, leading to his arrest;
e) XXXX left Honduras sometime around 2010 and claimed refugee status in Canada. His claim was accepted.5
f) On XXXX XXXX, 2018 while the family was away, there was a break-in at the claimant's house. No cash or valuables were taken, but the claimant testified that birth certificates for the family were missing;
g) The claimant fled Honduras for the U.S. on XXXX XXXX, 2018;
h) The claimant's spouse has not seen the Barrio 18 members at the church since she left the country;
NEXUS

[8] The Barrio 18 gang's interest in the claimant is criminal in nature. Her brother co-operated with police, resulting in the arrest and imprisonment of one of their members and they retaliated by murdering his son. They remain interested in locating the brother and are harassing his family members in an attempt to discern his whereabouts.

[9] Barrio 18 is a criminal gang. Threats are criminal acts and criminal acts do not generally form a nexus to the Convention.6

